<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><rss xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" version="2.0"><channel><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link><description>World News Today: Read the latest world news headlines, Current International breaking news world wide. In depth analysis and top news headlines world wide.</description><language>en-gb</language><lastBuildDate>2026-08-24T12:15:04+05:30</lastBuildDate><atom:link type="application/rss+xml" rel="self" href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/rssfeeds/296589292.cms"/><copyright>Copyright:(C) Mon, 24 Aug 2026 12:15:04 IST Bennett Coleman &amp; Co. Ltd, http://in.indiatimes.com/policyterms/1554651.cms</copyright><docs>http://syndication.indiatimes.com/</docs><image><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><url>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/507610.cms</url><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link></image><item><title><![CDATA[Mexico brings back bison 160 years after extinction; 47 animals are restoring the ecosystem]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133453681.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[After an absence of 160 years, American bison have made a remarkable comeback to Coahuila, Mexico. These majestic animals have become vital ecosystem engineers, actively rejuvenating worn-out landscapes. Their presence enhances soil quality and promotes water absorption in the dry terrain. Additionally, this return facilitates the resurgence of native predators and other wildlife, reflecting a wider initiative aimed at restoring vital species throughout North America.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/american-bison-had-been-hunted-to-extinction-in-mexico-160-years-later-the-country-introduced-47-bison-in-2025-and-the-ecosystem-is-slowly-restoring-itself/articleshow/133453681.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/american-bison-had-been-hunted-to-extinction-in-mexico-160-years-later-the-country-introduced-47-bison-in-2025-and-the-ecosystem-is-slowly-restoring-itself/articleshow/133453681.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T12:03:10+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133453766,imgsize-136470.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Chinese humanoid robots beat Usain Bolt’s 100m record and human high-jump record: Are humans losing?]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133451720.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[At the recent Beijing Robot Games, humanoid robots set remarkable new benchmarks in speed and agility. One robot sprinted 100 meters in an astonishing 9.39 seconds, shattering Usain Bolt's record. Another demonstrated incredible prowess with a standing high jump of 2.88 meters, surpassing human limits. These milestones reflect China's advancements in the robotics field, yet experts caution that widespread adoption remains a future prospect.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/chinese-humanoid-robots-just-beat-usain-bolts-100m-record-and-the-human-high-jump-record-are-humans-already-losing-to-machines/articleshow/133451720.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/chinese-humanoid-robots-just-beat-usain-bolts-100m-record-and-the-human-high-jump-record-are-humans-already-losing-to-machines/articleshow/133451720.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T12:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><encl